Durarara!! AU
Chapter 4 – Part 3
The two of them walked in silence for a full block before either spoke again.
Shizuo still had a firm grip on Curtis’s uninjured arm, mostly out of stubborn principle at this point. Blood continued to drip steadily from the head wound and the two stab marks in Curtis’s shoulder, leaving a sparse trail along the pavement. Neither of them seemed particularly bothered by the stares they drew from passersby.
Curtis eventually reached into his pocket with his free hand, pulled out a crumpled pack, and shook one cigarette free. He lit it one-handed with practiced ease, took a slow drag, then held the pack out toward Shizuo without looking at him.
“Smoke?”
Shizuo glanced sideways at the offer, still scowling, but the pure murderous edge from earlier had cooled into something closer to residual irritation. He eyed the pack for a second, then clicked his tongue and took one.
Curtis passed him the lighter.
Shizuo lit up, exhaled a sharp stream of smoke, and finally released Curtis’s arm as if the shared cigarette had settled some unspoken point between them. They continued walking side by side, two bloodied, dangerous-looking young men moving through the late afternoon streets of Ikebukuro like the violence of the past hour had only been a minor inconvenience.
“You’re still an idiot for talking about duct tape,” Shizuo muttered after a moment.
Curtis exhaled smoke through his nose, long black braids shifting with the motion.
“And you’re still an idiot for throwing trash cans at people.”
Neither of them smiled, but the tension between them had dropped into something almost workable.
They walked another half block in silence, smoke trailing behind them in the late afternoon air.
Shizuo took a drag from the cigarette Curtis had given him and exhaled slowly, eyes fixed ahead. The residual urge to punch something was still there—it always was—but it had settled into a lower, more manageable burn. The foreigner beside him was bleeding freely, had taken a full trash can and a board to the head, absorbed two stab wounds, and still walked like the biggest problem of his day was a ruined shirt.
That part was familiar.
Too familiar.
Shizuo had spent years watching people either fear him, use him, or treat his strength like a spectacle. Most people who could take a hit from him either crumpled or ran. The ones who didn’t usually had their own brand of wrongness—Shinra with his cheerful sadism, Celty with her headless quiet, even Vorona later on with her cold efficiency. But this one…
This one felt closer to something Shizuo recognized in himself.
The same short fuse.
The same way violence came too easily.
The same stubborn refusal to stay down even when staying down would have been smarter.
Shizuo glanced sideways at Curtis—at the blood-matted braids, the ruined white shirt, the calm way he smoked like the street fight had already been filed under “minor inconvenience.”
Something in his chest clicked with quiet, reluctant recognition.
This bastard’s the same kind of problem I am.
He didn’t say it out loud. He just took another drag and kept walking, the sense of familiarity settling in deeper than he was ready to admit.
Curtis took another drag from his cigarette and glanced sideways at the buildings they had already walked past.
“So where are you taking me?” he asked. “We passed the hospital and a clinic.”
Shizuo scoffed like the question itself was stupid.
“Those places ask too many questions,” he muttered, smoke spilling from his mouth. “You show up bleeding from a head wound and two stab marks, they call the cops. Then I’ve got to deal with explanations, and I hate explanations.”
He jerked his chin further down the street, toward the residential area where the buildings grew older and quieter.
“There’s a doctor who doesn’t care where the holes came from. He’s a pain in the ass, but he won’t call anyone. And he already deals with worse than you.”
Shizuo took another drag, then added flatly:
“Name’s Shinra. Try not to bleed on his floor more than you have to. He gets weirdly happy about that shit.”
Curtis raised an eyebrow at the description but didn’t argue. He simply kept walking, blood still dripping steadily onto the pavement, as the two of them headed toward the underground doctor who treated Ikebukuro’s strangest injuries.
Curtis exhaled a long stream of smoke and muttered under his breath.
“Greaaaat. Is he going to want to cut me open too?”
Shizuo turned his head and gave him a completely flat, deadpan look. No words. Just the expression of a man who had dealt with Shinra Kishitani for years and knew exactly how accurate that question was.
Curtis caught the look and sighed, long black braids shifting as he shook his head once.
“Of course he’s like that. Why wouldn’t he be.”
He took another drag from the cigarette, blood still dripping steadily from his shoulder and the side of his head, and kept walking like a man who had already accepted that the rest of his day was going to be inconvenient.
Shizuo almost—almost—looked amused. The expression vanished as quickly as it appeared, replaced by his usual scowl as they continued toward the apartment of Ikebukuro’s most enthusiastic underground doctor.
The apartment building was ordinary from the outside—old, slightly worn, the kind of place no one looked at twice. Shizuo led the way up the stairs without knocking, already familiar with the route, and shoved the door open like he paid rent there.
Curtis stepped in after him, mismatched eyes scanning the space. The place was lived-in, cluttered in a controlled way, with medical equipment mixed into the everyday mess of someone who treated injuries that official clinics preferred not to see.
“Nice place,” Curtis said simply, still bleeding onto the entryway floor.
A cheerful voice answered from further inside before Shizuo could reply.
“Well, well! What a rare combination of guests!”
Shinra Kishitani appeared from the hallway in his usual white coat, glasses catching the light, a bright and entirely inappropriate smile already spreading across his face as he took in the bloodied foreigner and the permanently irritated bartender.
His eyes lit up with genuine professional interest the moment they landed on the amount of damage Curtis was carrying.
“Ohhh. Head laceration, two puncture wounds to the shoulder, significant blood loss, and still standing upright.” Shinra clasped his hands together, delighted. “You bring me the most interesting patients, Shizuo.”
Shizuo scowled.
“Just fix him before he bleeds out on your floor and starts complaining about his shirt again.”
Curtis glanced at the smiling doctor, then at Shizuo, and already looked like he regretted every decision that had led him to this apartment.
Curtis looked down at the blood soaking through his white shirt and the dirt and scuffs on his uniform pants, then muttered under his breath with genuine regret.
“I should’ve gone in their pockets and made them pay for uniform replacement. Oh well.”
Shinra’s smile widened with pure delight.
“Priorities! I love it. Most people in your condition are too busy panicking about blood loss or organ damage, but you’re concerned about dry-cleaning. Wonderful.” He gestured toward a chair already prepped with medical supplies. “Sit. Shirt off. I want a clear look at those shoulder wounds before you decide duct tape is still a viable option.”
Shizuo snorted, arms crossed as he leaned against the wall.
“Told you he was an idiot about the clothes.”
Curtis shot him a flat look, then started unbuttoning what remained of the ruined white shirt with his uninjured hand, long black braids shifting as he moved. Blood continued to drip steadily onto Shinra’s floor.
“I’m not an idiot,” Curtis muttered. “I just don’t own that many shirts.”
Shinra laughed brightly, already snapping on a pair of gloves.
“Even better. A practical monster. This afternoon just keeps improving.”
Curtis didn’t fully remove the shirt.
He only pulled the bloodied fabric down and aside far enough to expose the two stab wounds on his shoulder, keeping the rest of his upper body covered. Even that limited view was enough.
Shinra’s cheerful expression shifted into something sharper and far more focused.
Under the fresh injuries, Curtis’s skin was mapped with scars.
Old ones. Layered ones. Some were thin and precise—blade work. Others were thicker, rougher, the kind left by teeth or claws. A few looked like they had been made by human hands. And interspersed among them were marks that didn’t cleanly belong to either category: irregular patterns, burns that didn’t match standard heat sources, and healed trauma that suggested things most doctors never saw outside of nightmares or classified reports.
Shinra leaned in slightly, eyes gleaming behind his glasses.
“…Oh my.” His voice had gone quieter, almost reverent. “That’s not a normal collection. Animals. Humans. And…” He tilted his head, studying a particularly strange set of scars near the collarbone. “Other things.”
Shizuo, still leaning against the wall, frowned as he caught a glimpse of the same mapped damage.
“The hell kind of life are you living, bastard?”
Curtis kept his gaze forward, expression unreadable as he held the shirt aside for treatment. The long black braids hung still against his back.
“A long one,” he said simply.
Shinra smiled again, but this time it carried genuine professional hunger.
“I’m going to enjoy working on you.”
Curtis’s voice cut through the room, flat and sharp.
“Stitch me up and don’t say a fucking word about my body. Stop staring.”
The temperature in the apartment dropped half a degree.
Shinra blinked once, then raised both gloved hands in mild surrender, though the fascinated gleam in his eyes didn’t fully disappear.
“Understood, understood. Professional silence it is.” He still sounded far too cheerful about being told off. “No commentary on the extensive personal history written across your skin. I’ll simply close the holes and pretend I didn’t see anything interesting.”
He clearly did not mean that last part.
Shizuo pushed off the wall slightly, arms still crossed.
“You’re the one who partially took the shirt off, idiot. Don’t get pissy when people notice you’ve been through a meat grinder.”
Curtis shot him a cold sideways look but didn’t waste energy responding. He kept the bloodied fabric held aside just enough for Shinra to work, mismatched eyes fixed forward, long black braids hanging still as the doctor finally stopped talking and reached for the suture kit.
Shinra hummed to himself as he began cleaning the stab wounds, the picture of obedience—while very obviously still cataloguing every scar he could see in his peripheral vision.
The front door opened again without a knock.
Celty stepped inside, the familiar black riding suit and yellow cat-eared helmet exactly as they had been the night before. The empty space where a head should have been was still somehow directed toward the room as she took in the scene: Shizuo leaning against the wall, Shinra in the middle of suturing, and the tall foreigner with the long black braids sitting partially shirtless, bloodied, and covered in more scars than most people earned in a lifetime.
She stopped just inside the entryway.
For a moment the apartment was quiet except for the soft sounds of Shinra working.
Celty’s helmet tilted slightly as she looked at Curtis—the same masked man from the rooftop and the street performance, now unmasked, injured, and sitting in her home. One gloved hand drifted toward the PDA at her side, but she didn’t type anything yet.
Shinra glanced over his shoulder with his usual bright smile, not pausing in his stitching.
“Welcome home, Celty! Perfect timing. Shizuo brought me a fascinating new patient. Try not to stare at the scar tissue—he’s already threatened me about it.”
Shizuo grunted in greeting but didn’t take his eyes off Curtis.
Curtis, still holding his ruined shirt aside for the needlework, met the dark visor of the yellow helmet with his mismatched eyes. The same calm recognition from their first meeting settled between them, now complicated by blood, stitches, and the fact that she had just walked in on far more of him than he usually allowed anyone to see.
Curtis let out a short, quiet sigh as Shinra continued working on the second stab wound.
“You don’t have to announce my scars,” he muttered, “but thanks for the stitches, doc.”
Shinra beamed, completely unfazed by the complaint.
“You’re very welcome! And don’t worry—I’m only announcing them to the people already in the room. Celty doesn’t count as gossip. She lives here.”
Celty’s yellow helmet remained angled toward Curtis. After a moment she lifted her PDA and typed a short message, turning the screen so he could read it without her needing to step closer.
You’re hurt worse than last night.
The statement was simple, observational. No exaggerated concern. No questions about the scars she could clearly see. Just the fact of his current condition, delivered in her usual straightforward way.
Shizuo watched the quiet exchange between the headless rider and the bloodied foreigner with narrowed eyes, clearly filing away the fact that the two already knew each other.
Curtis glanced at the PDA screen, then at the yellow helmet, and gave a small, tired nod of acknowledgment as Shinra tied off another suture.
Shinra’s eyes lit up with fresh interest as he glanced between Curtis and Celty, still working on the last of the sutures.
“Oh? You two already know each other?”
Curtis gave a short, humorless breath through his nose.
“Apparently wearing a mask doesn’t exactly hide my face.”
The dry comment hung in the room for a second.
Celty’s helmet tilted slightly. She typed on her PDA and turned the screen toward them.
The braids. And the way you move. The mask only covered so much.
Shinra looked delighted by the entire exchange.
“A masked foreigner with distinctive braids and a body full of mysterious scars, already acquainted with my dear Celty and capable of trading blows with Shizuo.” He tied off the final suture with a flourish. “Ikebukuro really does attract the most wonderful people.”
Shizuo scowled from his place against the wall.
“Wonderful isn’t the word I’d use.”
Curtis pulled the bloodied shirt back up over the freshly stitched shoulder with a slight wince, long black braids shifting as he adjusted the fabric. The head wound still needed attention, but the stab wounds were closed for now.
He glanced at Celty’s helmet again, then at the overly cheerful doctor, and looked like a man who was already regretting how many people in this city could recognize him.
Curtis rolled the freshly stitched shoulder once, testing it, then looked at Shinra.
“We good here?”
Shinra’s cheerful expression turned more serious as he examined the amount of blood still soaked into the white shirt and pooled on the floor.
“For the moment. But if you had lost much more, I would have insisted on a transfusion. Head wounds and deep punctures add up faster than most people realize.”
Curtis actually laughed—short, rough, and completely without humor.
“Yeah, I doubt that. The amount of times I bled out in the Appalachian forest and woke back up…” He shook his head, long black braids shifting with the motion. “Doubt a few butter knife scratches are going to put me in the ground. Life won’t let me off that easily.”
The apartment went quiet for a beat.
Shinra’s eyes gleamed with pure, unrestrained scientific interest behind his glasses. The casual mention of repeated fatal-level blood loss and waking up anyway was clearly the most exciting thing he had heard all day.
Shizuo’s frown deepened. The familiarity he had felt earlier settled heavier in his chest. People who talked about dying and coming back like it was an inconvenience were rarely ordinary—and rarely lucky.
Celty’s yellow helmet remained fixed on Curtis. After a moment she typed a short message and turned the PDA screen toward him.
Appalachian forest?
Curtis glanced at the screen, then at the three people watching him, and seemed to realize he had said more than he intended. He looked away first, expression closing off again as he reached for what remained of his ruined shirt.
Curtis didn’t elaborate.
He simply answered the PDA screen with a short, flat confirmation.
“Yeah. Appalachia.”
That was all.
The single word sat in the apartment like a door that had been opened half an inch and then deliberately left that way. No stories. No explanations about what had tried to kill him in those woods, how many times he had bled out, or what “waking back up” actually looked like. Just the place name and the implication that it had been enough.
Shinra looked like he was physically restraining himself from asking twenty follow-up questions at once. His fingers twitched against the suture kit with pure academic hunger.
Shizuo studied Curtis in silence, the sense of familiarity from earlier now mixed with something closer to wariness. People who survived the kind of damage the foreigner was describing rarely came out the other side normal—and Shizuo knew better than most what “not normal” could look like.
Celty’s helmet remained angled toward him. She typed again, the message shorter this time.
You don’t have to explain.
The restraint in the message was clear. She wasn’t pushing. After a moment she added a second line.
But you should stay off the streets until the head wound stops bleeding.
Curtis glanced at the PDA, then at the blood still seeping slowly from his scalp, and gave a small, noncommittal sound that could have meant agreement or simply acknowledgment. He finished adjusting the ruined shirt as best he could over the fresh stitches, long black braids hanging heavy against his back.
The conversation about Appalachia ended there—not because there was nothing left to say, but because he had decided not to say it.
Shinra disappeared into the back room with a vial of Curtis’s blood, humming to himself. The others barely had time to settle into the residual quiet before he returned—practically vibrating with excitement.
He was beaming.
“Oh, this is wonderful,” Shinra announced, holding up the results like a prize. “Truly, spectacularly wonderful. Do you have any idea how rare this is?”
Curtis glanced at him with flat suspicion.
“What.”
Shinra’s smile widened to an almost unnerving degree.
“Rh-null. Golden blood. No Rh antigens whatsoever. Do you understand how few people on the planet have this? You’re a medical unicorn. A walking statistical impossibility.” He looked genuinely moved. “I could publish papers. I could retire on the research potential alone.”
Shizuo pushed off the wall slightly, frowning harder.
“The hell does that mean in normal words?”
“It means,” Shinra said brightly, “that your new friend here has one of the rarest blood types in existence. Compatible with almost no one, and invaluable to almost everyone who studies this sort of thing. Combined with the scar tissue, the repeated near-fatal blood loss, and the fact that he’s still walking around after today’s damage…” He turned his delighted gaze back on Curtis. “You are, biologically speaking, fascinating.”
Celty’s helmet tilted toward Curtis, the dark visor unreadable.
Curtis stared at Shinra for a long second, then let out a slow, tired breath through his nose.
“…Great,” he muttered. “Another thing that makes me a problem.”
Shinra only looked happier.
Curtis leaned back in the chair, long black braids shifting against the ruined white shirt as he processed the information.
“That explains why when I was younger, I needed a blood transfusion and my parents said no.” His voice stayed even, almost detached. “They had to pay out of pocket. They said I would be fine…”
He paused, then lifted one hand in a dry, half-hearted thumbs up.
“…and I was.”
The gesture was so casually delivered after everything else he had revealed that it only made the statement land harder.
Shinra looked like he had just been handed a second Christmas.
“Your body compensated. Of course it did. Repeated extreme blood loss, rare blood type, scar tissue that suggests trauma no child should survive—and yet here you are, still walking around after taking a board to the skull and two stab wounds like they were minor inconveniences.” He adjusted his glasses, eyes shining. “Your parents were either extremely confident or extremely desperate. Possibly both.”
Shizuo’s expression had gone darker. The familiarity he felt toward Curtis was starting to feel less like recognition and more like looking into a warped mirror.
Celty typed a short message and turned the PDA toward Curtis.
Your parents knew.
It wasn’t a question.
Curtis glanced at the screen, then at the three people watching him, and lowered the thumbs-up hand without further comment. The apartment felt smaller under the weight of how much he had already given away in a single afternoon.
Curtis’s tone never changed.
He delivered the next part the same way he had said everything else—flat, almost conversational, like he was commenting on the weather.
“If I complained, they would hit me. If I tripped and fell, I would have to apologize. Standard things.” A short pause. “Probably why they abandoned me in the Appalachian forest.”
The apartment went still.
He didn’t give the silence time to settle.
Curtis pushed himself up from the chair, long black braids shifting against the bloodied shirt as he tested his balance. The fresh stitches pulled, the head wound still seeped, but he stood like none of it was relevant.
“Anyways, guys. It’s been real.” He gave a small, empty nod toward the room in general. “I got some wandering to do.”
He was already turning toward the door.
Shinra’s cheerful mask had slipped into something closer to genuine concern—or at least intense, complicated interest. “Wait—you can’t just—”
Shizuo straightened fully from the wall, the earlier familiarity now edged with something darker. “Oi. Sit your ass back down. You’re still bleeding.”
Celty moved first.
She stepped into his path, yellow helmet angled up at him, one gloved hand lifting not to grab but to block the way. The PDA screen lit up a moment later with a short, firm message.
You’re not leaving like this.
Curtis stopped in front of her, mismatched eyes meeting the dark visor. For a second the old, closed-off calm tried to settle back into place—but the weight of what he had just said still hung in the room, too heavy to pretend hadn’t been spoken.
Curtis tilted his head slightly, long black braids shifting with the motion. The question came out genuine, almost confused—like he truly didn’t understand the problem.
“Why? I’m stitched up.”
The simplicity of it made the silence that followed heavier.
Celty’s helmet remained fixed on him. She typed quickly, the PDA screen turning toward his face with more force than usual.
Your head is still bleeding.
You just told us your parents abandoned you in a forest.
Sit down.
Shizuo’s voice came rougher, less patient.
“Because you’re still dripping blood on the floor, you just trauma-dumped half your childhood, and you’re acting like walking out the door is a normal next step.” He jabbed a finger toward the chair Curtis had left. “Sit. Down.”
Shinra, for once, didn’t sound cheerful.
“Curtis-kun. Rh-null blood, repeated near-death blood loss, a body full of scars that shouldn’t exist on someone still walking, and now this.” He adjusted his glasses, expression unusually serious. “You are not ‘stitched up.’ You are a medical and psychological event. Please sit before I have Shizuo make you sit.”
Curtis stood there between the door and the three of them, mismatched eyes moving from Celty’s PDA to Shizuo’s glare to Shinra’s uncharacteristically firm expression. The old instinct to leave—to keep moving, to not stay long enough for people to look too closely—was clearly still active.
But for the first time since entering the apartment, he didn’t immediately argue.
Curtis’s expression barely changed.
“Trauma? I don’t get it.” He said the word like it was foreign, something that didn’t apply to him. “Anyways, you’re all freaking out over nothing. My head will be fine.”
The dismissal was so complete it almost sounded sincere.
Celty’s gloved fingers tightened around the PDA. She typed harder than before and turned the screen toward him with clear insistence.
It is not nothing.
You are still bleeding.
Sit down.
Shizuo looked one second away from physically putting him back in the chair.
“You don’t ‘get’ trauma?” he repeated, voice low and incredulous. “Your parents beat you, made you apologize for falling down, then dumped you in a forest to die—and you’re standing here telling us it’s nothing because the holes got closed?” He took a step closer. “Sit your ass down before I make you sit down.”
Shinra exhaled slowly, the last of his usual cheer thinning into something more clinical and concerned.
“Curtis. The fact that you genuinely don’t register any of this as abnormal is, in itself, the problem.” He gestured toward the chair with the suture kit still in hand. “Your head wound is still open. Your blood volume is low. And you just described childhood abuse and abandonment like you were listing groceries. Please. Sit.”
Curtis remained standing, mismatched eyes moving between the three of them. The instinct to brush it off—to keep the weight of it small enough to carry alone—was still written clearly across his face.
But the combined wall of Celty, Shizuo, and Shinra wasn’t moving.
Curtis held their stares for another second longer, then finally moved.
He walked back to the chair and sat down with a short, reluctant exhale, long black braids settling against the backrest. The bloodied shirt pulled tight across the fresh stitches as he leaned back, mismatched eyes still carrying that same flat practicality.
“Okay. I’m sitting.” A small shrug. “What now? ’Cause there was this neat corner bar I wanted to hit up after I go home and change.”
The complete lack of transition between “childhood abandonment” and “plans for a drink” left a fresh wave of silence in the apartment.
Shinra pinched the bridge of his nose beneath his glasses.
“You are not going to a bar.”
Shizuo stared at him like he had grown a second head.
“You just got stabbed, boarded, and nearly bled out, and your next thought is alcohol?”
Celty typed with clear, pointed energy and turned the PDA toward him.
No bar.
You are staying here until the head wound is closed and Shinra says you can leave.
Also change your shirt. You look like a crime scene.
Curtis glanced at the screen, then at the two men flanking the headless rider, and let out a long, put-upon sigh—like the real inconvenience of the day was being forced to sit still instead of the blood loss or the trauma dump.
“Fine,” he muttered. “No bar. For now.”
He didn’t sound particularly convinced.
Curtis gestured vaguely at the blood-soaked white fabric still clinging to him.
“My shirts are at home. And I’m not taking my shirt off.”
The second half of the statement was firmer than the first—clear, final, and non-negotiable. After everything they had already seen of the scar tissue, he had no intention of giving them a full view.
Shinra opened his mouth, then closed it again, clearly weighing how hard to push. Professional curiosity warred with the fact that Curtis had already threatened him once about commenting on the scars.
“…Understood,” Shinra said at last, though he sounded mildly disappointed. “We can work around the shirt. The head wound is the priority right now anyway.”
Shizuo scowled.
“You’re still bleeding through it. At least let him cut the damn thing off you so it stops sticking to the stitches.”
Celty typed and turned the PDA toward Curtis.
You can keep the shirt on.
But you are not leaving until the head wound is treated.
Where is your home?
The question was practical. If his clean clothes were there, someone would eventually need to know where “there” was.
Curtis glanced at the screen, then at the three of them, and looked like a man realizing that sitting down had only been the first of several concessions he was going to be forced into.
Shinra moved behind the chair with fresh gloves and a clean suture kit.
“Hold still. Head wounds bleed dramatically, but this one needs proper closure.”
Curtis sat rigid as the doctor carefully parted the long black braids, working around the thick plaits to expose the laceration left by the 2x4. Blood had matted sections of hair against the scalp. As Shinra cleaned the area and shaved a minimal path for the needle, more of the skin beneath became visible.
There were scars here too.
Older than the fresh wound. Some thin and silvered with age, others thicker and more irregular. A few looked like they had been made by blades. Others suggested blunt trauma that had healed poorly. One particular mark curved along the side of his head in a way that didn’t match any standard accident. The braids had hidden most of them until now.
Shinra’s hands paused for half a second.
He didn’t comment—Curtis had already warned him—but the silence itself was telling. The doctor’s eyes flicked once toward Shizuo and Celty before he resumed work with deliberate professionalism, stitching the fresh laceration closed with steady, precise movements.
Shizuo’s jaw tightened as he caught sight of the older damage beneath the hair.
Celty’s helmet remained angled downward, the dark visor fixed on the exposed section of scalp. She didn’t type. She simply watched, the same quiet, heavy attention she had given the scars on his shoulder earlier.
Curtis stared straight ahead, expression locked down, long braids held carefully out of Shinra’s way. He didn’t acknowledge the additional evidence of his past written across his scalp. He simply sat there and let the needle work, as if the new layer of scars being revealed changed nothing about the conversation they had already stopped having.
